00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:40.990
it is always exclusively

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.370
what I would call a business decision.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:45.203
Hmm.

00:10:45.203 --> 00:10:46.167
Okay?

00:10:46.167 --> 00:10:50.460
It means I am writing this
song as part of the mythical

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.020
thing that me and you have built together.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.450
And it is obviously going
to be for the purpose,

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:55.860
it's comedy song.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:57.630
We could use this in
something or whatever,

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:00.210
or I need to write a song
for Buddy System soundtrack

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.260
or something like that, right?

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:02.093
Right.

00:11:02.093 --> 00:11:03.900
Which I think is the last
time we were really intensely

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.810
writing songs was Buddy System Season two.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:07.643
And it was so.

00:11:07.643 --> 00:11:09.690
Actually it was the music for the tour.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.310
That's right.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:12.357
We wrote the few songs.

00:11:12.357 --> 00:11:13.380
We came back and we wrote.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.390
The few new songs that we like,

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:19.350
Why I Travel, Kyle, The Shirt Song.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:21.000
Which interestingly enough, like,

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.078
so Buddy System season two,

00:11:23.078 --> 00:11:25.020
it was very collaborative.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.020
It was very last minute to the point

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.030
where we were going on separate vacations,

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.590
right before filming.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:34.470
And we had to divide up songs,
something we had never done.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:36.510
Yeah, 'cause we were so under the gun.

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.640
We were so under the gun.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.350
I had to write one song.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:41.277
You had to write another song.

00:11:41.277 --> 00:11:43.260
And we had to commit that we were both

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.191
gonna bring them back basically done.

00:11:45.191 --> 00:11:46.024
Yeah.

00:11:46.024 --> 00:11:47.970
Which was unusual.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:49.803
And then for the tour,

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:56.073
the Rhett and Link Live Tour in 2019,

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:58.830
you wanted to start playing piano.

00:11:58.830 --> 00:11:59.790
And so then you were like,

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:02.340
you made yourself write
Why I Travel on the piano.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:03.483
That really wasn't.

00:12:04.976 --> 00:12:07.050
I didn't add too much to that
song as much as I recall,

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:09.540
I don't recall doing much to it.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:12.090
And then in the other ones like Kyle,

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.940
that was a little more collaborative.

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:17.913
And The Shirt Song just isn't memorable.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:20.060
I mean, it had memorable.

00:12:21.107 --> 00:12:22.890
The Shirt Song is a performance art.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:24.870
It's not really a song
that you wanna listen to.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:25.703
Yeah.

00:12:25.703 --> 00:12:26.536
It's a song.

00:12:26.536 --> 00:12:27.641
But those.

00:12:27.641 --> 00:12:28.474
It's a song you might wanna watch after.

00:12:28.474 --> 00:12:29.340
You just kind of went
away and wrote those,

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:30.660
'cause again we were in this divide

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:33.363
and conquer mode and music,

00:12:35.096 --> 00:12:36.450
and the tour was kinda like

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:37.745
get in where you fit in kind of thing.

00:12:37.745 --> 00:12:38.578
Yeah.

00:12:38.578 --> 00:12:40.620
So there was a little bit
of, even in that yeah.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:41.453
You would sit,

00:12:41.453 --> 00:12:44.040
you started more and more initiating

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.930
Rhett and Link songs on your own.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:46.763
Yeah.

00:12:47.762 --> 00:12:49.860
But this time in 2019, you sat down,

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.430
you weren't thinking
about a business move,

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:55.470
you weren't thinking about an obligation,

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:56.303
a creative obligation.

00:12:56.303 --> 00:12:58.563
I was like, I've got this idea.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:01.530
And really it wasn't like
I'm going to sit down

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:02.363
and write a song.

00:13:02.363 --> 00:13:03.660
It was like, I'm playing the guitar.

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.980
And I kind of like what I'm hearing here

00:13:07.704 --> 00:13:09.330
and I'm just starting to
kind of sing a melody.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.640
Usually I'll sing a melody
that is nonsensical.

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.140
Words that just sound good

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.870
and I'll make a voice memo
just so it's just like,

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:18.030
oh, I wanna remember this
melody that I came up with.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:20.460
And you sing words that sound good,

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:21.293
that don't make sense, right?

00:13:21.293 --> 00:13:23.550
It's a very common songwriting technique.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:26.373
And so, but I was like,

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:31.500
I was saying words that did make sense.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:35.613
And I realized that I was
writing a song to God.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:37.770
Okay?

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:39.930
So writing a song to God.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:40.763
Oh.

00:13:40.763 --> 00:13:42.330
And when I say writing a song to God,

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.400
as someone who doesn't
necessarily believe in God

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:45.233
at this point,

00:13:47.537 --> 00:13:50.340
I was writing a song to the idea

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.980
that I had of God in the past.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:54.240
And you know,

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:59.240
basically writing a song like
I had a relationship with you.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:00.300
So if you don't know.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:01.980
Do you remember the first line

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:03.723
that kind of came out of it?

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.503
I still remember when we used to talk,

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:09.600
you never said much,

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.070
but I knew what you thought.

00:14:11.070 --> 00:14:12.420
Hmm.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:13.253
Huh.

00:14:14.893 --> 00:14:16.740
So that was the first line that came out.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.190
And so I was like, oh,
this is kind of like,

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:21.630
if you don't,

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:23.490
if you're not a Christian
or a former Christian

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:24.810
or a spiritual person,

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:27.900
maybe you wouldn't pick up
on the thematic imagery here.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:28.860
And you might think I'm just writing

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:30.090
a song about a lost love,

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.440
because it's about a
relationship that you had.

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:34.830
I've got these old letters,

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:35.910
it's called Old Letters.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:38.220
I've got these old letters
that you wrote to me.

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.920
And I sit down and read
'em, the Bible, you know?

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.450
I don't like giving all the,

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:43.830
I want you to listen to the music

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:46.010
and interpret it for yourself.

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:47.960
But this one's pretty obvious, I guess.

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:51.256
But I was writing it and I was like,

00:14:51.256 --> 00:14:52.410
I don't know who this is for.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:53.807
I don't know why.

00:14:53.807 --> 00:14:54.640
I don't know.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:56.100
I'm writing a song because I'm going

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:57.480
to have a music project one day.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.250
It was very much just like,

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:03.570
oh this is coming out of
me in a very easy way.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:04.403
And I'm not,

00:15:04.403 --> 00:15:07.050
it's interesting 'cause
I'm not trying to be funny.

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:09.660
There's no jokes in this song.

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.360
It's very serious and it's very sincere.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:13.867
Hmm.

00:15:13.867 --> 00:15:14.700
And it happened very quickly.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:16.530
That's one of the things about almost all,

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:18.480
there's 11 songs on the finished album

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.490
and all of them.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.070
The process between beginning
to write and then record

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:24.480
or like fine tuning the lyrics,

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:26.130
that may have been a months long process.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:28.500
But with rare exception,

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:30.720
the writing of the music and
the first pass of the song

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:31.553
is usually something that happens

00:15:31.553 --> 00:15:35.130
when I just sit down and do
it in about one to two hours.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:35.963
And then you go,

00:15:35.963 --> 00:15:37.290
I've got a song, right?

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:38.850
I'm gonna come back,
I'll change that lyric.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:40.750
I'll work on that, whatever.

00:15:40.750 --> 00:15:42.570
But they usually come out really easy.

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:45.030
If I sit down and it's this hard I'm like,

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:46.980
I'm doing this for fun.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:48.390
It's not a business decision.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:49.230
It's not a job.

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:51.573
So there's no forced creative.

00:15:52.531 --> 00:15:54.360
So this first song.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:55.500
Letters.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:56.333
Yeah.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:03.780
It pretty much came, it
formed in that sitting.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.080
And then what?

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:07.800
So I sat down and like recorded,

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.270
I recorded a demo of it,

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:10.350
but I wouldn't have called it a demo.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:11.183
I was just like,

00:16:11.183 --> 00:16:12.430
oh, I'm gonna record this.

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:13.650
And I actually recorded

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:17.100
it in like the Evernote audio record.

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:18.090
I don't know if you've ever done this,

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:22.110
but like Evernote has, you
can press add audio and it,

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:23.850
so it is so low quality.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:25.500
It sounds like something like out

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.150
of the twenties compared to like,

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:30.663
if you just do a voice memo
and attach it, interestingly.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:34.350
And I was just like, okay, I did that.

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:35.643
Now this is 2019.

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:42.050
At some point after that, I
would say months after that,

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:44.220
I just kind of told my therapist about it.

00:16:45.411 --> 00:16:47.850
I was like, yeah, I wrote,

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:49.050
we were having lots.

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:49.890
Can you pull it up?

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:51.690
Play a little bit of it.

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:52.560
I don't wanna do that.

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:54.384
'Cause that's not, that's not the.

00:16:54.384 --> 00:16:55.217
That's not the single.

00:16:55.217 --> 00:16:56.050
Okay.

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:56.910
It's actually the last song on the album.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:57.743
Okay.

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:00.540
Maybe I'll play that when,

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:01.500
'cause you can see how different

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.480
the final version ended
up being, or maybe,

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:04.380
That's fair.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:05.873
Or once the album comes out.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:08.790
But I I'm talking to
my therapist about it.

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:11.500
And we were having a lot
of broader conversations

00:17:12.961 --> 00:17:14.130
about me getting into a place

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:17.160
where I was in creative flow
and him just making rec,

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:20.703
just recognizing like
seems like when you are,

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:24.430
you're healthy and you're thriving

00:17:25.285 --> 00:17:27.480
and you're experiencing joy,
when you're in a creative flow.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.200
Now obviously I'm in a creative flow a lot

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:33.120
with what we do for a living,

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:34.980
because this is a creative business.

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:36.900
This is a content business.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.231
So that I'm doing lots of writing.

00:17:39.231 --> 00:17:41.610
I'm doing a lot of creative things,

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.670
but it's also a very
sophisticated business

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:45.970
with a lot of moving parts

00:17:47.276 --> 00:17:49.200
and those business
parts, which I do think,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.090
that both of us are really good at,

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:53.440
isn't necessarily life giving

00:17:54.453 --> 00:17:55.350
and doesn't breathe a lot of joy.

00:17:55.350 --> 00:17:57.060
It's kind of like, well,
if you wanna do this right,

00:17:57.060 --> 00:17:57.980
you gotta do this right.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:58.950
So you gotta answer

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:00.420
these business questions,
etcetera, etcetera.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:01.253
Yeah.

00:18:01.253 --> 00:18:03.270
For other people business
can get 'em into flow,

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:04.740
but that's not really our thing.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:05.573
Yeah.

00:18:06.835 --> 00:18:08.730
And so he would,

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:11.220
my therapist without giving specifics

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.670
was just like saying like,
yeah, you should just,

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:16.230
I'm telling him about writing a song,

00:18:16.230 --> 00:18:18.180
he's like, when you talk about that,

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.784
I kind of see you light up a little bit.

00:18:20.784 --> 00:18:23.234
So I would just encourage
you to continue to just

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:28.800
express yourself creatively
and move towards things

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:30.090
where you can express yourself creatively.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.493
He wasn't being like, go do an album.

00:18:35.896 --> 00:18:37.860
So it wasn't like, okay,
yes, I'm gonna do that.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:38.863
It was like, yeah, yeah.

00:18:38.863 --> 00:18:39.840
I really enjoyed that.

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:41.340
Maybe I'll do it again.

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:43.208
Pandemic roles around, 2020.

00:18:43.208 --> 00:18:44.041
Oh yeah.

00:18:45.428 --> 00:18:47.940
And our lives changed
drastically like everyone else's.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:49.350
We were obviously still really busy.

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:51.000
We were doing everything that
we were doing with Mythical

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.820
and trying to keep all
our content streams going,

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:57.783
but you know, less
travel more time at home.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:01.350
I got this guitar in my bedroom.

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:02.640
I got a guitar at the Creative House.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:03.570
I got a guitar here.

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:06.139
I got guitars everywhere, right?

00:19:06.139 --> 00:19:06.972
And so I just found myself

00:19:06.972 --> 00:19:09.180
as a way of sort of like passing the time,

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:12.630
picking up my guitar
and beginning to play.

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:15.720
And I just started finding
that every time I did that,

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:17.430
a song would come outta nowhere, right?

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:18.263
Hmm.

00:19:20.426 --> 00:19:22.050
And so then, before I knew it,

00:19:22.050 --> 00:19:24.963
I had written a song to my parents.

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:29.820
I had written a song to my children.

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:32.253
I had written a song to my wife.

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:37.260
I talked about the song that
I wrote for Valentine's D.ay

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:38.850
We talked about on the podcast.

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:39.683
Yeah.

00:19:39.683 --> 00:19:40.516
That I played for her,

00:19:40.516 --> 00:19:43.263
which is a song that I
ended up writing for her.

00:19:44.490 --> 00:19:45.323
Oh yeah.

00:19:45.323 --> 00:19:46.434
You're talking,

00:19:46.434 --> 00:19:47.267
you're not talking about
when you were dating.

00:19:47.267 --> 00:19:48.180
You're talking about like.

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.350
I did it when I was dating too.

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:50.280
I'm talking about like.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:51.916
You did a 2020.

00:19:51.916 --> 00:19:55.110
It's probably 2021 Valentine's Day

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:56.910
is when I probably talked about it.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:57.743
Okay.

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:00.960
What was happening was

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:03.270
is without any real calculation,

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:05.580
all the songs that I was
writing were more like,

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:09.960
it was a cathartic sort of therapeutic way

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.330
of thinking about my deconstruction.

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:14.400
So I point out those songs
and one to my parents,

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:17.940
one of my kids, one of my wife as,

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:22.380
what was happening with me
is I've had this very long

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.570
process of deconstruction.

00:20:24.570 --> 00:20:26.550
And as I've talked about many times,

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.970
my spiritual deconstruction on its surface

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:31.950
is it was a very
intellectual thing, right?

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:33.930
I wasn't wronged by someone emotionally.

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:35.700
I didn't have a pastor do me wrong.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:36.663
I wasn't like,

00:20:37.621 --> 00:20:40.080
I didn't have the rug pulled
out from underneath me.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.510
It was just a very slow process

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:44.070
of access to new information

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:47.100
that was increasingly more
difficult to reconcile

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:51.780
with my Christian worldview,
to the breaking point.

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:52.770
Right?

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:55.950
But that doesn't mean that
there weren't incredibly heavy

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:57.810
emotional things happening,

00:20:57.810 --> 00:20:59.193
relational things,

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.810
like deconstructing from a faith
that your parents gave you.

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:05.910
And they still have.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:08.640
Being a father to kids
when they were born.

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:10.080
You're taking them up
in front of the church

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:12.390
and dedicating them to God

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:14.790
and then no longer believing in that God

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:16.920
in the same way and no longer adhering

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.360
to a biblical or Christian

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.190
worldview and still
wanting the best for them.

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:21.060
Like what is that?

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:24.240
So what I would find is that
the way for me to process

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:27.000
all these emotions was
to write a song about it.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.080
So I started talking more

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.250
and more to my therapist about this.

00:21:29.250 --> 00:21:30.120
And he was like,

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:32.020
this is bringing something out in you,

00:21:33.055 --> 00:21:34.200
and just started talking
to Jessie about it.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:35.050
And she was like,

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:38.940
it's not,

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:43.940
anyone who knows me well knows that it's,

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:47.143
I am a very emotional person,

00:21:47.143 --> 00:21:48.864
but on the surface

00:21:48.864 --> 00:21:50.075
you don't see that,

00:21:50.075 --> 00:21:52.260
but there's a lot going on.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:54.270
You gotta kind of tap
into it and you gotta,

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:56.160
it has to come out in a
certain way for you to be like,

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:59.550
oh, this dude was feeling
a whole lot of things.

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:00.383
Hm hm.

00:22:02.144 --> 00:22:04.200
And so I just kept going and going.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:08.519
And again, the thought as
I was writing these songs

00:22:08.519 --> 00:22:11.400
was this is a therapeutic
cathartic process.

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:14.823
Now of course I am a
full-time entertainer.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:19.740
And so as I'm getting to like
the third and fourth song,

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:23.280
I'm like, I'm making something here.

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:26.190
Yes, I'm doing this for myself.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:27.840
Yes, it is a therapeutic process.

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:29.910
Yes, I do feel like
there's like an emotional

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:32.640
release every time I do this,

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.500
but I'm making something here.

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:36.390
It's kind of, yeah.

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:38.673
There's at tension of,

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:42.780
you're doing something for therapy,

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:44.223
but then you're also,

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:46.590
you're not just a creative person,

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:48.063
but you are an entertainer.

00:22:49.463 --> 00:22:50.296
And so it's, I mean,

00:22:50.296 --> 00:22:52.800
there's echoes of the conversation
that we had about like,

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.690
why you're growing your
hair out like this,

00:22:54.690 --> 00:22:58.295
it originated in therapy
and you were like,

00:22:58.295 --> 00:22:59.940
it was a therapeutic exercise,

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:00.810
but at a certain point,

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:03.840
you're like, I actually
think I will look good

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:04.890
with my hair like this.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:06.600
I think my wife will like me better.

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:09.690
I think that this fits into my brand.

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.790
These things are things that
you can't help but think about

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:14.505
because of the position
that you have in life.

00:23:14.505 --> 00:23:15.338
Well, and not just that,

00:23:15.338 --> 00:23:17.520
it's also my personality
as an Enneagram three,

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:18.510
which I don't,

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:19.443
not everybody,

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:22.153
some people get tired of
hearing about the Enneagram.

00:23:22.153 --> 00:23:23.130
I get it.

00:23:23.130 --> 00:23:25.500
But the Enneagram three,
just think about it this way.

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:27.210
It just means your performance minded.

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:30.030
It means that you find
your value and your worth

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:35.030
in what you perform for
people, in winning, right?

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:36.087
Right, so there's.

00:23:36.087 --> 00:23:38.400
And it's very unhealthy.

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:39.750
It can be very unhealthy.

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:42.270
And it goes with you into every endeavor,

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:47.040
even like something that
like nothing for anybody is,

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:49.290
is like absolutely pure.

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.630
It's like, we're not, you know?

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:52.598
Yeah.

00:23:52.598 --> 00:23:54.990
But that doesn't mean
you can't be aware of it

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:56.550
and still like,

00:23:56.550 --> 00:24:01.550
I believe that this can be
a relatively pure endeavor.

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:05.250
Well, it's funny 'cause I have a song

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:06.450
that I've already written.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:08.520
I've written multiple additional songs

00:24:08.520 --> 00:24:09.723
that are not on the album

00:24:09.723 --> 00:24:11.610
that I guess will be for
something in the future.

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:14.430
But one of them is about
this exact process.

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:15.360
Huh.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:16.310
That talks about,

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:19.170
I ain't sure what I'm trying to prove

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:20.820
or who I'm trying to prove it to.

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:22.793
You know what I'm saying?

00:24:22.793 --> 00:24:26.100
Like that's the treadmill that
the three is on constantly.

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:27.510
Which is really me.

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:29.010
I wrote it when we,

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:30.780
I finished the the album

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.460
and I'm thinking about this process

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.087
that I'm in right now, which you know,

00:24:34.087 --> 00:24:36.780
'cause I've been telling you about it.

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:38.940
It's one thing to write the music

00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:41.190
and I love the process of writing.

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:42.690
I love the process of recording.

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:44.820
I love the process of
collaborating with talented people

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:46.740
who make what I wrote better

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:49.080
than it would be if I
was the one in charge

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:51.120
from the beginning to the end.

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:52.263
I love all that.

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:54.780
But as I get into the
process of like, okay,

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:57.300
well you have to like have a
bio and you gotta have like,

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:58.750
you gotta get pictures taken.

00:25:00.783 --> 00:25:02.310
Once I started beginning to
do these things that feel very

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:05.070
performative and marketing oriented

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:08.790
is when I started thinking
about the built-in

00:25:08.790 --> 00:25:13.650
irony of doing something
that is personal, cathartic,

00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:16.230
therapeutic, beneficial to me

00:25:16.230 --> 00:25:18.903
and then turning it into
a consumable product,

00:25:20.088 --> 00:25:23.550
which is checking all the
threes performance boxes.

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:25.293
So I wrote a song about that,

00:25:26.220 --> 00:25:27.971
but it's not on the album

00:25:27.971 --> 00:25:31.133
because it's about the
process of doing this, right?

00:25:31.133 --> 00:25:32.956
But just to go back.

00:25:32.956 --> 00:25:33.789
Yeah.

00:25:33.789 --> 00:25:36.993
So at a certain point you had
amassed a number of songs.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:39.587
Like what was,

00:25:39.587 --> 00:25:42.229
you made a decision that you
were gonna make this an album?

00:25:42.229 --> 00:25:43.860
Well, when I got to like four songs,

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:44.940
I was like, ah,

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:46.980
two more songs and I've got like an EP.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:49.238
I mean you can do a four song EP,

00:25:49.238 --> 00:25:53.010
but I was like a six song
EP, like that's respectable.

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:53.883
That's something.

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:55.590
I can write two.

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:58.350
So, but that process was very
much, it wasn't calculated.

00:25:58.350 --> 00:25:59.183
It was like,

00:25:59.183 --> 00:26:01.140
as I would keep talking about this stuff

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:03.570
with my therapist and with Jessie,

00:26:03.570 --> 00:26:05.370
I'd be like you know,

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:09.300
I feel like these songs need
to be released into the wild.

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:10.800
They need to be put into the world.

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:12.423
And here's my thinking on that.

00:26:14.301 --> 00:26:17.920
So, you know that I'm a
big fan of David Bazan

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:20.493
Pedro the Lion,

00:26:22.986 --> 00:26:24.227
he's probably even more
known for his band.

00:26:24.227 --> 00:26:25.377
That's his band name.

00:26:28.555 --> 00:26:31.530
So he is a guy that grew
up in a Christian home

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:35.550
and was very much a
strong, very similar story.

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:37.860
And then he deconstructed

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:39.630
and he was actually making
like Christian music,

00:26:39.630 --> 00:26:41.220
I think back in the day
with Pedro the Lion.

00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:43.920
And then of course he
deconstructed and it kind of,

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:46.530
his music evolved with his worldview,

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.433
but he made an album
probably in the late, alts,

00:26:51.173 --> 00:26:52.006
like 2008, 2009,

00:26:52.006 --> 00:26:54.900
called Curse the Branches.

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:57.350
It was curse your branches
or curse the branches.

00:27:01.004 --> 00:27:03.330
And it was essentially his
deconstruction album, right?

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:05.650
And it was a little bit of an angry album

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:08.790
in that he was kind of like, he was,

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:11.910
a little bit angry at the
world that he had come from,

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:14.970
but also it was just deeply.

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:16.440
It was so thoughtful.

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:20.250
And so like specifically
connecting to what I,

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:21.893
now I wasn't listening
to it in 2008, 2009,

00:27:21.893 --> 00:27:22.890
when it came out,

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:26.580
I listened to it probably
almost 10 years later.

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:27.930
Yeah, I listened to it once.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:28.763
And.

00:27:28.763 --> 00:27:32.040
It just, yeah, it was a bit of a downer.

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:33.753
Yeah, but I was just like man,

00:27:34.860 --> 00:27:36.480
this is so meaningful to me

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:39.090
because of my particular experience.

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:39.923
Yeah.

00:27:39.923 --> 00:27:41.130
And I was like you know,

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:43.470
I'd love to make the album

00:27:43.470 --> 00:27:46.020
that I would want to listen to, right?

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:46.853
I've heard that before,

00:27:46.853 --> 00:27:49.350
write the book that
you would want to read.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.623
So I was like, okay, I'm
gonna make this music.

00:27:52.623 --> 00:27:55.410
It's the music that I would
want to listen to as someone

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:56.970
who's kind of been through

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:58.833
or going through what I went through.

00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:01.140
And so then it was like,

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:04.233
this could be a gift to
those people who have,

00:28:05.130 --> 00:28:07.320
who can relate to this story.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:09.600
And not just people who, again,

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:11.430
this is a super niche thing.

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:14.280
Again, it's very much a country album,

00:28:14.280 --> 00:28:15.780
which is not necessarily

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:18.543
like the most popular genre of music,

00:28:20.638 --> 00:28:23.757
but it's the genre of
music that I can personally

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:27.300
write and sing in a credible way.

00:28:27.300 --> 00:28:29.793
Let's get more into that
after listening to it.

00:28:32.576 --> 00:28:35.553
But it's also personally
my favorite type of music.

00:28:37.604 --> 00:28:41.640
But the subject matter is
very, at least for this,

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:44.130
this album is very
specific because every song

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:45.693
is related to that process.

00:28:45.693 --> 00:28:46.526
Yeah.

00:28:46.526 --> 00:28:48.120
And again, that's when it
became this thing like,

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:49.500
well, this should be something

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:52.300
that is released because

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:55.710
it's so deeply personal and emotional.

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:58.110
And I know that there's so
many people out there going.

00:28:58.110 --> 00:28:59.190
This is a cultural movement.

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:00.630
We talked about it many times.

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:03.720
People reevaluating their
traditional worldviews,

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:05.340
whatever your traditional worldview,

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:07.320
doesn't have to be
Christianity specifically.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.530
But like we're in an era
where you're bombarded

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:11.640
with so many perspectives

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:13.290
and so much information that it's becoming

00:29:13.290 --> 00:29:16.140
increasingly difficult to
hold onto any traditional view

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:18.570
that requires putting your
blinders on and blocking out

00:29:18.570 --> 00:29:20.340
everyone else's perspective.

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:23.040
And so this is a cultural upheaval,

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:25.680
which I think ultimately
will be for the best.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:27.090
But so many people can relate that,

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:29.640
I hope that regardless
of their circumstances,

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:30.840
it'll be like, ah, this,

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:31.920
you can connect with this music

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:36.900
and it can be a gift to people, you know?

00:29:36.900 --> 00:29:39.750
So that's when it became
like I'm gonna release this,

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:42.003
but that was all that happened.

00:29:43.103 --> 00:29:47.520
But of course I hadn't
recorded anything beyond demos.

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:50.400
And so this is the second
piece of the puzzle,

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:51.420
because I didn't know how

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:53.550
it was gonna become a thing, right?

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:56.283
We developed a relationship
with Derek Furman,

00:29:57.473 --> 00:29:59.220
who was Britton's producer on your cousin,

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:01.473
Britton Buchanan on his stuff.

00:30:03.529 --> 00:30:04.362
And he started producing our music

00:30:04.362 --> 00:30:05.820
for the ethical society.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:08.910
The Lionel Richie stuff,
the Brooks &amp; Dunn stuff,

00:30:08.910 --> 00:30:11.040
the Hazel, the Hazel project.

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:12.180
Yeah.

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:13.770
And just love the dude

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:16.860
and just love the way that
he commits to a project

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:18.960
and just is super technical
and just like really

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:21.480
gets involved and can do,

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:24.502
can execute very well
in a number of genres.

00:30:24.502 --> 00:30:26.040
So I was just like talking
to him and I was like,

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:29.253
hey, I got these songs
that I've been writing.

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:35.280
What would it look like if you
recorded and produced them?

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:37.800
And of course, he was
like, I love this idea.

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:42.513
And so getting someone else
involved in the project.

00:30:42.513 --> 00:30:43.560
Yeah.

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:45.000
That light's a fire.

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:47.490
And then you begin moving forward

00:30:47.490 --> 00:30:49.990
and it moves from just
being a hobby and a pastime

00:30:50.918 --> 00:30:51.751
A production.

00:30:51.751 --> 00:30:52.618
This is good.

00:30:52.618 --> 00:30:53.700
This is gonna be a thing.

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:56.460
Again, from a timeline standpoint.

00:30:56.460 --> 00:30:57.660
I made that commitment last year.

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:59.133
So that was 2021.

00:31:00.218 --> 00:31:01.051
Was talking to him about it,

00:31:01.051 --> 00:31:01.884
like the middle of the year.

00:31:01.884 --> 00:31:03.210
And I was like, I don't know
when this is gonna come out.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:04.470
Probably 2023.

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:05.303
That's what I told him.

00:31:05.303 --> 00:31:07.980
I was like, I don't want
you to prioritize this.

00:31:07.980 --> 00:31:12.173
I cannot prioritize this because
I can't work on this 'cause

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:15.360
it's very, very busy.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.190
Have a lot of other things going on.

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:19.330
This is a nights and weekends thing for me

00:31:20.672 --> 00:31:21.505
and if you want it to be a nights

00:31:21.505 --> 00:31:22.980
and weekends thing for
you, then I was like,

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:26.310
don't stop working on
something to work on my thing.

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:28.620
But I'll send you things as I have 'em.

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:30.120
Send you demos.

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:31.620
So that was kinda the process,

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:35.880
but he would like turn
something around and like,

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:37.740
I'd be like, man,

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:40.533
that sounds a lot better
than my demo, you know?

00:31:43.695 --> 00:31:46.440
And I kind of got addicted to the process.

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:49.200
And I was, why am I
only gonna do six songs?

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:50.280
I got more songs.

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:52.260
And I was like, there's
a lot of different.

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:54.250
The thing that sent me to 11 songs

00:31:55.182 --> 00:31:58.080
was trying to cover the
breadth of the experience.

00:31:58.080 --> 00:31:59.550
I was like, well,
there's this other aspect

00:31:59.550 --> 00:32:01.183
that I really want to talk about?

00:32:01.183 --> 00:32:02.880
And then I was like,

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:05.730
also, I kind of feel that
there needs to be like a thesis

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:09.123
statement that is sort of
the beginning of the album.

00:32:09.123 --> 00:32:11.220
It's the first song,

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:14.670
which is the first single
and that's believe me,

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:15.690
which was one of the,

00:32:15.690 --> 00:32:17.310
was written pretty late in the process

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:18.210
because it was stepping back

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:19.680
and looking at what I
had done and thinking

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:22.380
about this would be a
good thesis statement,

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:26.520
a good opener to where I'm
gonna kind of take you through

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:28.413
conceptually on the album.

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:32.344
And so that's how I ended
up getting to 11 songs.

00:32:32.344 --> 00:32:33.567
Okay.

00:32:33.567 --> 00:32:35.040
And the last thing I'll talk about

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.833
before we actually listen to the single,

00:32:41.216 --> 00:32:42.437
is why it's called James
and the Shame, right?

00:32:42.437 --> 00:32:44.733
Why is it not Rhett McLaughlin?

00:32:47.769 --> 00:32:50.670
So at some point early
on, I had this idea that,

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:52.470
well, what if this was like a covert thing

00:32:52.470 --> 00:32:55.533
that I didn't even talk about,
but I just put out there.

00:32:56.880 --> 00:32:58.650
And then I started thinking about the fact

00:32:58.650 --> 00:33:01.110
that so much of,

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:04.470
if the whole point of this
thing is to connect with people

00:33:04.470 --> 00:33:08.040
who are experiencing something
similar or can relate,

00:33:08.040 --> 00:33:10.413
well, one of the things
that has been such a,

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:13.320
has brought those people
out of the woodwork

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:15.340
is me sharing these things through

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:21.060
the public forum that I have
as the public person that I am.

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:22.170
Yeah.

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:23.331
And so I was like,

00:33:23.331 --> 00:33:24.420
it doesn't make sense to not talk about it

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:25.253
or try to make it seem

00:33:25.253 --> 00:33:26.940
like there's just some new country guy's

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:28.440
talking about spiritual things.

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:30.440
And if you wanna know about it, you can.

00:33:31.710 --> 00:33:36.710
But I was like, but I don't
want it to be Rhett McLaughlin.

00:33:36.840 --> 00:33:39.630
I don't want it to be my name.

00:33:39.630 --> 00:33:41.340
I want it to be something that is like,

00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:43.560
hey, this is something outside

00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:45.240
of everything else that this guy does.

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:47.370
When you hear the name, Rhett McLaughlin,

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:51.060
you think Rhett and Link comedy.

00:33:51.060 --> 00:33:53.250
I'm not supposed to take this seriously.

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:54.360
When is the joke?

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:56.670
When is the first punchline
coming in this song?

00:33:56.670 --> 00:33:57.503
Right?

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:00.300
And also, I don't.

00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:02.130
It becomes pretty obvious pretty quickly.

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:04.200
And I didn't want it to be
a distraction for somebody

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:07.230
who stumbles across it in a playlist

00:34:07.230 --> 00:34:09.933
or something on Spotify to
be like Rhett McLaughlin?

00:34:11.974 --> 00:34:14.190
If what I have put out into
the world professionally,

00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:15.690
which is a bunch of lighthearted,

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:17.880
silly stuff to take your mind off things,

00:34:17.880 --> 00:34:19.050
isn't your cup of tea.

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:21.210
I don't want you to not
wanna listen to this,

00:34:21.210 --> 00:34:23.280
if this would actually be
something you'd be into.

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:24.726
Yeah.

00:34:24.726 --> 00:34:27.690
So that's where James
and the Shame came from.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:31.650
James and the Shame,
not only does it rhyme,

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:34.830
it kind of roll off the tongue, but again,

00:34:34.830 --> 00:34:38.460
shame is the dominant sort of emotion

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:40.666
of the Enneagram Three,

00:34:40.666 --> 00:34:42.263
of someone who is performance minded.

00:34:43.804 --> 00:34:44.637
So the twos,

00:34:44.637 --> 00:34:47.070
the threes and the fours in the Enneagram

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:49.143
are all in the heart triad.

00:34:52.223 --> 00:34:55.530
And that means that there's
a whole lot going on in here

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:58.560
that they try to deal
with in certain ways.

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.000
And the way the three
deals with their shame

00:35:02.103 --> 00:35:03.743
is to try to keep performing for you.

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:07.320
Don't, just keep watching,
watch me do cool things.

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:09.390
And you won't have to know who I really am

00:35:09.390 --> 00:35:10.590
and who I'm ashamed of.

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:12.210
Hmm.

00:35:12.210 --> 00:35:15.450
And so that whole idea of
James and the Shame is like,

00:35:15.450 --> 00:35:19.740
obviously the shame means two things.

00:35:19.740 --> 00:35:23.400
Number one, this was not
a, it is a solo project.

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:27.000
I wrote all of the music
and all of the lyrics,

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:30.090
but the final production
includes some other very talented

00:35:30.090 --> 00:35:32.310
musicians who played
different instruments.

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:34.710
And so I guess in some ways
that represents the other people

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:37.110
who collaborated to make
these final products,

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.090
but also more so than that,

00:35:39.090 --> 00:35:41.790
it's the shame that follows
me around wherever I go.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:44.870
It's like, you got James
you got the shame too.

